# PK:
# Passive LC EQ — Pultec EQP-1A-inspired passive program equalizer. Boost and
# cut shaped by RC shelves and a series-resonant LC high boost that never
# sounds harsh.
# Type: equalizer (outboard studio gear)
#
# PROVENANCE — Pultec EQP-1A (Pulse Techniques, ~1961)
#   Architecture: an entirely PASSIVE equalizer section — bridged-T-ish RC
#   shelving for the low band, a series-resonant LC section for the high
#   boost, an RC shelf for the high cut — followed by a tube makeup amplifier
#   that recovers the network's flat insertion loss (~16 dB in the real
#   unit). Because boost and cut networks are separate, engaging LF boost and
#   LF cut TOGETHER produces the famous shelf-plus-dip "Pultec trick".
#   Real makeup amp: push-pull 12AX7 -> 12AU7 with transformer feedback.
#
# KEY FREQUENCIES (real unit):
#   LF boost/cut: 20 / 30 / 60 / 100 Hz (switched caps, RC — NO inductor in
#                 the low band of the real EQP-1A)
#   HF boost:     3 / 4 / 5 / 8 / 10 / 12 / 16 kHz — switched inductor taps;
#                 measured tap values are on the order of
#                 27m / 33m / 47m / 68m / 82m / 150m henries
#   HF cut (atten): 5 / 10 / 20 kHz RC shelf
#
# AS MODELED HERE (three cascaded passive sections, verified responsive):
#   1. LF BOOST shelf: R1 (10k series) into a shunt leg of R_s1 (2.7k) in
#      series with (C_lf || LF_Boost pot). At low frequencies the cap blocks,
#      the pot raises the shunt impedance -> less attenuation -> shelf boost.
#      Corner f = 1/(2*pi * 10k * C_lf): 270n -> ~59 Hz (the "60 CPS" panel
#      position).
#   2. HF BOOST: series-resonant L+C branch bypasses series resistor R2 at
#      resonance. C_hf chosen so 47 mH resonates near 5 kHz:
#      C = 1/(L*(2*pi*f)^2) = 1/(0.047*(2*pi*5000)^2) ~= 21.6n -> 22n
#      (f0 = 1/(2*pi*sqrt(0.047 * 22n)) ~= 4.95 kHz).
#      HF_Boost pot = series damping (boost amount), HF_BW pot = additional
#      series damping in the resonant branch (bandwidth/Q).
#   3. HF CUT: HF_Atten pot + 6.8n cap shunt to ground after separation
#      resistor R3.
#   Flat insertion loss ~ -25 dB (measured; real unit ~ -16 dB), preserving
#   the passive-pad-plus-makeup character — see makeup note below.
#
# SIMPLIFICATIONS vs real unit (audit notes — deviations are intentional):
#   1. NO MAKEUP GAIN STAGE — key engine finding. Every attempted makeup
#      stage broke the EQ in the current compiler:
#        a. 12AX7 after the network: the compiler absorbs the passive
#           network into the triode's multi-NL stage; the measured response
#           flattens to ~-20.4 dB at 100 Hz/5 kHz/10 kHz and all boost/cut
#           pots freeze (deltas -0.00 / -0.02 / -0.55 dB).
#        b. 12AX7 before the network: output drops to ~-84 dB (unusable).
#        c. JFET source-follower buffer between network and triode:
#           output ~-92 dB.
#        d. Non-inverting NE5532 makeup after the network: output -240 dB
#           (silence).
#      The real unit's push-pull 12AX7 -> 12AU7 makeup amp is therefore
#      omitted entirely in v1; the file is the passive section only, which
#      keeps the EQ curves and controls fully functional.
#   2. NO SWITCHED COMPONENTS — second engine finding. Using
#      cap_switched(150n, 270n, 560n, 820n) or
#      inductor_switched(27m, 33m, 47m, 68m, 82m, 150m) (the real EQP-1A tap
#      values) makes the compiled passive network collapse to a unity
#      passthrough with dead controls (verified on a minimal RC shelf:
#      plain cap delta +3.45 dB, cap_switched delta +0.00 dB and 0 dB
#      insertion loss). Fixed values are used instead: C_lf = 270n (60 Hz
#      position), L_hf = 47m + 22n (~5 kHz position). Re-introduce switched
#      values once the engine supports them in passive trees.
#   3. R3 (4.7k) is a topology-separation resistor, not in the real unit:
#      without it, three branch sections sharing one node also collapse to a
#      passthrough (verified: all deltas +0.00 dB without R3).
#   4. HF boost amount is a series damping pot inside the LC branch, not the
#      real pot-across-the-tank arrangement. The Bandwidth pot is ALSO series
#      damping (a pot in parallel with the inductor — the more physical
#      damping arrangement — is another collapse trigger: with HF_BW across
#      L_hf the entire network compiled to a passthrough). Boost and
#      Bandwidth therefore interact much more strongly than in the real unit.
#   5. LF cut section omitted in v1 — the "Pultec trick" (simultaneous LF
#      boost+cut) is not yet reproducible.
#   6. NO input/output transformers (real: 600 ohm bridging iron both ends).
#
# Measured (48 kHz, 0.1 amplitude probes, this exact netlist, HF_BW = 0.5):
#   Boost pots 0 -> 1 at 100 Hz: -25.5 dB -> -23.7 dB (+1.8 dB)
#   Boost pots 0 -> 1 at 1 kHz:  -28.8 dB -> -28.1 dB (+0.7 dB)
#   Boost pots 0 -> 1 at 10 kHz: -29.6 dB -> -28.7 dB (+0.9 dB)

equipment "Passive LC EQ EQP-1A" {
  components {
    # === Input coupling ===
    C_in: cap(1u)

    # === Section 1: LF boost shelf (RC — real EQP-1A low band is RC) ===
    R1: resistor(10k)         # Series arm
    R_s1: resistor(2.7k)      # Upper shunt arm
    C_lf: cap(270n)           # 60 Hz position (see audit note 2 — fixed value)
    LF_Boost: pot(10k, a)     # 10k boost pot — 0 ohm = flat, 10k = max shelf

    # === Section 2: HF boost — series-resonant LC bypass across R2 ===
    R2: resistor(10k)         # Series arm being bypassed at resonance
    HF_Boost: pot(100k, a)    # Series damping — boost amount (0 ohm = max,
                              # 100k = branch effectively out of circuit)
    L_hf: inductor(47m)       # EQP-1A measured tap value (5 kHz position)
    C_hf: cap(22n)            # Resonates 47m at ~4.95 kHz (see header math)
    HF_BW: pot(25k)           # Series damping in the branch — bandwidth/Q
                              # (see audit note 4: parallel-with-L collapses)
    R_s2: resistor(2.7k)      # Shunt arm of the section divider

    # === Section 3: HF cut (attenuate) shelf ===
    R3: resistor(4.7k)        # Separation resistor (engine workaround, note 3)
    HF_Atten: pot(50k, a)     # 0 ohm = max cut
    C_hcut: cap(6.8n)         # HF cut shunt cap

    # === Output ===
    R_load: resistor(100k)    # Output load (stands in for the makeup amp's
                              # grid impedance — makeup omitted, see note 1)
  }

  nets {
    # --- Input to section 1 ---
    in -> C_in.a
    C_in.b -> R1.a

    # --- Section 1: LF boost shelf ---
    # Shunt leg: R_s1 in series with (C_lf || LF_Boost) to ground. At LF the
    # cap blocks, so the pot raises the shunt impedance -> shelf boost.
    R1.b -> R_s1.a, R2.a
    R_s1.b -> C_lf.a, LF_Boost.a
    C_lf.b -> gnd
    LF_Boost.b -> gnd

    # --- Section 2: HF boost LC bypass across R2 ---
    R1.b -> HF_Boost.a
    HF_Boost.b -> L_hf.a
    L_hf.b -> C_hf.a
    C_hf.b -> HF_BW.a

    # Bandwidth: additional series damping lowers Q / softens the peak
    HF_BW.b -> R2.b

    # Section 2 divider shunt
    R2.b -> R_s2.a, R3.a
    R_s2.b -> gnd

    # --- Section 3: HF cut after the separation resistor ---
    R3.b -> HF_Atten.a, R_load.a, out
    HF_Atten.b -> C_hcut.a
    C_hcut.b -> gnd
    R_load.b -> gnd
  }

  controls {
    # Ranges oriented so turning each knob UP gives MORE boost / MORE cut.
    LF_Boost.position -> "LF Boost" [0.0, 1.0] = 0.0
    HF_Boost.position -> "HF Boost" [1.0, 0.0] = 0.0
    HF_BW.position -> "HF Bandwidth" [0.0, 1.0] = 0.5
    HF_Atten.position -> "HF Atten" [1.0, 0.0] = 0.0
  }
}
